From: Dirk Nehring <dnehring@gmx.net>
Subject: [PATCH] device: attach the transfer player at the channel switch,
 let the TS buffer consumer re-check every 10 ms

Two delays sit between a live channel switch and the first packet that
reaches the output device, both in device.c:

1. cDevice::SetChannel() launches the transfer control, but only the main
loop attaches it (cControl::Attach() at the top of every iteration).
Until then cTransfer::Receive() drops every TS packet. How long that
takes depends on what the main loop is doing: after a key press it first
draws the channel display (skins with signal bars query the frontend,
300-400 ms right after a retune); after SVDRP CHAN it is usually asleep
in cRemote::Get(). Measured: 300-900 ms.

The late attach can also switch the channel twice: SVDRP CHAN runs
SetChannel() in the SVDRP thread under mutexChannel. A main loop that
reaches the "visible programme" check meanwhile waits in HasProgramme()
and then finds the launched but unattached control - no player, no PIDs
on the primary device - so it calls Channels->SwitchTo() for the same
channel again.

SetChannel() now attaches the control right after launching it. The
launch already happens after the track state is set up, and Launch()
and Attach() both take the control mutex, so that ordering stays intact.
Attach() is a no-op for an attached control: the main loop's call, and
plugins that attach from cStatus::ChannelSwitch() as a workaround, are
unaffected.

2. cTSBuffer's ring signals its consumer only once it is 10% full, which
live traffic rarely reaches, so cDevice::Action() waits for the 100 ms
get timeout: the first packets of a new channel reach the receivers in
100 ms steps, and a receiver that was told to stop notices it up to
100 ms late. The consumer now re-checks every 10 ms; the extra wake-ups
cost no measurable CPU time.

Same-transponder zaps via SVDRP CHAN, 39 per build, time to the first
video PES at the output device: 816 -> 256 ms with the attach, and
239 -> 155 ms more with the TS buffer timeout (key zaps 230 -> 145 ms).
Key zaps that retune or need the CAM gain little, the tuner or CAM is
slower than the main loop there.

---
--- a/device.c
+++ b/device.c
@@ -976,9 +976,14 @@
      // Launch() makes the control visible to the main loop, which attaches it and
      // starts feeding TS data into the primary device - if that happens while this
      // function is still setting up the track state above, the two threads race
-     // on availableTracks et al. (SwitchChannel() may run in the SVDRP thread):
-     if (NeedsTransferMode)
+     // on availableTracks et al. (SwitchChannel() may run in the SVDRP thread).
+     // Attach it right here instead of waiting for the main loop: until then
+     // cTransfer drops all TS data, and HasProgramme() reports no programme, which
+     // can make the main loop switch to the channel a second time:
+     if (NeedsTransferMode) {
         cControl::Launch(new cTransferControl(Device, Channel));
+        cControl::Attach();
+        }
      cStatus::MsgChannelSwitch(this, Channel->Number(), LiveView); // only report status if channel switch successful
      }
 
@@ -2021,7 +2026,7 @@
   deviceNumber = DeviceNumber;
   delivered = 0;
   ringBuffer = new cRingBufferLinear(Size, TS_SIZE, true, "TS");
-  ringBuffer->SetTimeouts(100, 100);
+  ringBuffer->SetTimeouts(100, 10); // the ring signals Get() only above 10% fill, too late for live TV
   ringBuffer->SetIoThrottle();
   Start();
 }
